title: Planner choices measured and not built
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: rationale
summary: Two cost choices the query planner was weighed against and left out — a page under a large subtree, a two-term osm path — measured warm in October 2026 and cold in November: neither alternative reads less.
audience: dev

Two cost choices of the [[Query planner]] were weighed against measurements and left
out; they need not be measured again unless the repositories grow by an order of
magnitude. Both were weighed warm, a
random read priced at ≈ 2 µs, then re-measured cold on 9 November 2026 (`L`, 105 000
nodes; how: [[Query planner]]), where a scattered read costs ≈ 120 µs: both still hold.

* ''An unsorted page under a large subtree.'' `follows_transitive` reads the root's whole descendant bitmap where a page wants a hundred ids. Warm (7 October 2026, `query.folder` against `query.page`, a folder holding the whole repository): +0.08 ms on 21 000 nodes, +0.4 ms on 105 000, about 4 ms extrapolated to a million. The alternative — a per-candidate ancestry check stopping at the page — costs ≈ 100 × depth × 2 µs ≈ 1 ms, so it loses below roughly 300 000 nodes, and its walk cannot be budgeted without the subtree's size, which the store does not keep. Cold: `/dir0` (104 999 files) 47–62 ms against 44–51 ms for the plain page, 251 keys against 247; the ancestry check would read ≈ 100 × depth scattered keys, ≈ 36 ms at a depth of 3 — it loses by more cold than warm.
* ''A two-term `osm` path whose earlier term is common'' (the bench's finder): ≈ 5 ms warm on 105 000 nodes, nearly all of it each anchor's ancestry. Cold: `dir1 file12` 577–688 ms, 4 805 keys read before the index runs; the single term `file12`, which the index serves natively, 421–438 ms for 3 350 keys. The order-sensitive part costs ≈ 1.4 times the name search itself, and both are the price of ≈ 1 100 scattered nodes (1 111 names hold `file12`, as many `dir1`; 111 paths match). The bitmap formulation — the anchors within the descendants of the nodes naming the earlier term — reads those 1 111 `dir1` nodes and a descendant key each: no fewer keys, as scattered.

[[The session scenario]] (a day of GUI use on 50 000 files) showed no query over its
budget: every list, finder and folder step under 1 s on a neutral machine.

